{"name":"Monkey Madness","hex":"#63584c","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"monkey","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A member of the clade Simiiformes other than those in the clade Hominoidea containing apes, generally (but not universally) distinguished by small size, tails, and cheek pouches.","Any simian, including humans."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To meddle; to mess (with).","To mimic; to ape."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/monkey"},{"term":"madness","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The state of being mad; insanity; mental disease.","The state of being angry."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/madness"}],"entries":[{"title":"Monkey","blocks":[{"text":"Monkey is a common name that may refer to most mammals of the infraorder Simiiformes, also known as simians. Traditionally, all animals in the group now known as simians are counted as monkeys except the apes. Thus monkeys, in that sense, constitute an incomplete paraphyletic grouping; alternatively, if apes (Hominoidea) are included, monkeys and simians are synonyms."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/Ubud_Monkey_Family.jpg/330px-Ubud_Monkey_Family.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monkey","wikidata":"Q1367"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/BrownSpiderMonkey_%28edit2%29.jpg/500px-BrownSpiderMonkey_%28edit2%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/BrownSpiderMonkey_%28edit2%29.jpg/1280px-BrownSpiderMonkey_%28edit2%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/BrownSpiderMonkey_%28edit2%29.jpg/500px-BrownSpiderMonkey_%28edit2%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser